{"name":"Hyacinth","hex":"#936ca7","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"Hyacinth","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["Alternative form of Hyacinthus.","A male given name from Ancient Greek."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/Hyacinth"}],"entries":[{"title":"Hyacinth","blocks":[{"text":"Hyacinthus is a genus of bulbous herbs and spring-blooming perennials. They are fragrant flowering plants in the family Asparagaceae, subfamily Scilloideae and are commonly called hyacinths. The genus is native predominantly to the Eastern Mediterranean region from the south of Turkey to the Palestine region, although naturalized more widely."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7e/Hyacinth_-_Anglesey_Abbey.jpg/330px-Hyacinth_-_Anglesey_Abbey.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacinth","wikidata":"Q158758"},{"title":"Hyacinth (mythology)","blocks":[{"text":"Hyacinth or Hyacinthus is a deified hero and a lover of Apollo in Greek mythology. His cult at Amyclae southwest of Sparta dates from the Mycenaean era. The hero is mythically linked to local cults and identified with Apollo. In the Classical period, a temenos (sanctuary) grew up around what was alleged to be his burial mound, which was located at the feet of a statue of Apollo. He is described as a Spartan prince who was also admired by Zephyrus, Boreas, and Thamyris. Scholars suggest he was originally a pre-Hellenic vegetation deity representing the natural cycle of decay and renewal. He is variously said to be the son of Clio, one of the Muses, by Pierus, Oebalus, or Amyclas."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f7/Eros_youth_Met_28.167.jpg/330px-Eros_youth_Met_28.167.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hyacinth_(mythology)","wikidata":"Q207273"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https